<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anubis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Inpu, Anpu, Anoup</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anubis is the god of funerary rites, protector of graves and guide to the underworld in ancient Egyptian religion, usually depicted as a canine or as a man with a canine head; Anubis is the Greek form of his Egyptian name, Anpu or Inpu. A jackal god, probably Anubis, appears in stone inscriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hor-aha">Hor-Aha</a> and Djer, and in the Old Kingdom he was the most important god of the dead, a role <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> took over in the Middle Kingdom. He was also an embalmer and attended the scale at the Weighing of the Heart, which decided whether a soul could enter the realm of the dead. He was depicted in black, a colour of regeneration and of the soil of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Since Predynastic Egypt, when the dead were buried in shallow graves, jackals had been strongly associated with cemeteries, because as scavengers they uncovered bodies. A jackal was chosen to protect the dead on the principle of fighting like with like. In the Early Dynastic period Anubis was portrayed in full animal form, and in the Middle Kingdom often as a man with the head of a jackal.</p><p>His parentage varied: early mythology made him a son of Ra, the Coffin Texts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a> a son of Hesat or Bastet, and later tradition more commonly the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>. Plutarch reported that he was the illegitimate son of Nephthys and Osiris, adopted by Isis, a story one scholar sees as an attempt to bring an independent god into the Osirian pantheon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Anubis acted in several roles. As He who is in the place of embalming he was patron of embalmers, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> myth he helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> embalm the god's body. As foremost of the westerners and lord of the sacred land he protected graves and cemeteries, which were usually on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. The Jumilhac papyrus tells that he subdued Set, who had turned himself into a leopard to attack the body of Osiris, and wore Set's skin as a warning to anyone who would desecrate tombs.</p><p>In the weighing of the heart in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-dead">Book of the Dead</a> he was Guardian of the Scales, weighing the heart against ma'at, often shown as an ostrich feather. A soul heavier than the feather was devoured by Ammit. His colour, black, stood for the discolouration of a corpse after embalming, for the fertile silt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and for rebirth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Few major myths involved Anubis, yet he was among the most frequently depicted gods and most tomb inscriptions carried prayers to him; his cult centre was at Cynopolis, and he was revered throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In the Ptolemaic period he was merged with the Greek god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a> as Hermanubis, since both guided souls to the afterlife. Greek writers of the Roman period called him a psychopomp.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> typically scorned animal-headed Egyptian gods, and Anubis was mockingly called Barker, but he was sometimes linked with Sirius, Cerberus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hades">Hades</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> swear oaths by the dog of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Apuleius's Golden Ass shows his worship continuing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to at least the 2nd century CE, and Hermanubis appears in alchemical and hermetic literature of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anubis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The catacombs dedicated to the jackal god <b>Anubis</b> at Saqqara held some eight million mummified animals, most of them dogs, offered by pilgrims to the god.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Weighing of the Heart, shown in Spell 125, <b>Anubis</b> leads the dead before Osiris, they recite the Negative Confession, and their heart is weighed against Maat; a heart out of balance is eaten by Ammit, the Devourer.</blockquote><a href="/work/book-of-the-dead">Book of the Dead · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
